      "math_academy_id": "q-170502",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f",
      "knowledge_point": "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial",
      "problem": "Factor $4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${w}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81 & ={(2{w}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (2{w}^{2})\\cdot 9+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{w}^{2}+9)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+9 & =2{w}^{2}-(-9) \\\\  & =2{w}^{2}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w)^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81 & =(2{w}^{2}+9)^{2} \\\\  & =((\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i}))^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
