      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Sum of a Complex Number and Its Conjugate",
      "problem": "If $z=-9-\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-9-\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=-9,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(-9) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-9-\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-9+\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(-9-\\text{i})+(-9+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-9+(-9))+\\text{i}(-1+1) \\\\  & =-18+\\text{i}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
